table definitions
=================

In Postgres, the definition of a catalog table is spread across
multiple files.  The basic structure of the table is usually defined
in an eponymous header file.  Any indexes on that table are defined in
indexing.h.  If the table uses "large" columns, it requires a separate
toast table in toasting.c|h.  If the table is shared, then
catalog.c:isSharedRelation() must list the table and its associated
indexes, and toast table and toast indexes if they exist.  Finally, any
new catalog table header must be listed in the
Postgres .bki source file dependencies in the catalog Makefile.

Foreign key relationships in system catalogs are declared using
BKI_LOOKUP and DECLARE_FOREIGN_KEY annotations in the catalog header
files.  genbki.pl processes these to generate system_fk_info.h,
which is used by the pg_get_catalog_foreign_keys() SQL function.

Single-column OID references to another catalog should be marked with
BKI_LOOKUP(catalog) on the column definition.  Use BKI_LOOKUP_OPT if
the column can be zero (InvalidOid) instead of a valid reference.

For example:
        regproc         compcompressor BKI_LOOKUP(pg_proc);
        Oid             segrelid BKI_LOOKUP_OPT(pg_class);

Multi-column foreign keys or non-OID references require explicit
DECLARE_FOREIGN_KEY annotations.  For example:

        DECLARE_FOREIGN_KEY((attrelid, attnum), pg_attribute, (attrelid, attnum));

See the upstream src/include/catalog/genbki.h for the full list of
foreign key declaration macros.

